-- title:
--   Two full right ideals of a maximal quaternion order form a free rank-two module
-- statement:
--   Let $c, d \in \mathbb{Q}$ and let $q$ be a prime such that the predicate `IsDefiniteRamifiedExactlyAt c d q` holds for $H = \mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q}, c, d]$, i.e. $c < 0$, $d < 0$, and for every height-one prime $v$ of $\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{Q}}$ the completed algebra $H \otimes_{\mathbb{Q}} \mathbb{Q}_v$ is a division ring (every nonzero element is a unit) precisely when $q$ lies in the prime ideal attached to $v$. Let $O$ be a $\mathbb{Z}$-submodule of $H$ which is a maximal order in the sense of the project: $O$ contains $1$, is closed under multiplication, spans $H$ over $\mathbb{Q}$, is finitely generated as a $\mathbb{Z}$-module, and every $\mathbb{Z}$-submodule with these four properties that contains $O$ equals $O$. Let $I_1, I_2$ be $\mathbb{Z}$-submodules of $H$, each contained in $O$, each stable under right multiplication by $O$ ($z \in I_k$, $o \in O$ imply $zo \in I_k$), and each full in the sense that there is a nonzero integer $n$ with $n \cdot o \in I_k$ for all $o \in O$. Then there exist matrices $\gamma, \gamma' \in M_2(H)$ with $\gamma\gamma' = 1$ and $\gamma'\gamma = 1$ such that for every column vector $x : \mathrm{Fin}\,2 \to H$, all coordinates of $\gamma' x$ lie in $O$ if and only if $x_0 \in I_1$ and $x_1 \in I_2$.
--
--   This is the case of a direct sum of two full right ideals of Eichler's theorem that locally free modules of rank at least two over a maximal order in a definite rational quaternion algebra are free: the conclusion says that $I_1 \times I_2 = \gamma\,O^2$ inside $H^2$, so that $I_1 \oplus I_2$ is free of rank two as a right $O$-module, even though $I_1$ and $I_2$ need not themselves be principal. It feeds the construction of a basis-free description of such ideals, and is used in the project's results producing a single invertible matrix matching $O$-membership conditions and in the statement that a full right ideal is generated by finitely many elements in the appropriate one-sided sense.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_QuaternionAlgebra_IsMaximalOrder_exists_matrix_forall_mulVec_mem_iff_of_le.lean

